<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1334–<a href="/years/1369">1369</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Peter the Cruel, Peter I of Castile, Pedro I of Castile</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, called the Cruel, was King of Castile and León from 1350 to 1369. His reign was marked by conflict with the nobility and a civil war against his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry of Trastámara</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, while Peter was aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward the Black Prince</a>, who won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-najera">Battle of Nájera</a> for him in 1367. Henry killed him at Montiel in 1369. Peter built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcazar-of-seville">Alcázar of Seville</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile.jpg" alt="Peter of Castile" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre I (1334-1369; roi de Castille et de León). Autorité émettrice de monnaie; Séville (Espagne; atelier monétaire). Atelier monétaire · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Espagne,_Castille_et_Leon,_Pierre_I,_dobla,_S%C3%A9ville_-_btv1b11335305r_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter was born on 30 August 1334 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile">Alfonso XI of Castile</a> and Maria of Portugal. He was to marry Joan, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, but she died of the plague in 1348 on her way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. He began his reign when almost sixteen, at first under the control of his mother and encouraged by the Portuguese minister Count Alburquerque. He was the last ruler of the main branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anscarids">House of Ivrea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the summer of 1353 Peter married Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> and two days later abandoned her for María de Padilla. In 1355 he married Juana de Castro, persuading her that his marriage to Blanche was void, and deserted her after two nights. Blanche died in 1361, and accounts of her death differ: French historians say Peter had her murdered, others that she was poisoned or shot with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a>, though plague is possible. Peter was excommunicated by Pope Urban V for his persecutions of the clergy. From 1356 to 1366 he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-two-peters">War of the Two Peters</a> with Aragon, whose king supported Peter's bastard brothers. In this period he carried out the series of murders that made him notorious.</p><p>In 1366 his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry of Trastámara</a> invaded with soldiers of fortune including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-du-guesclin">Bertrand du Guesclin</a> and Hugh Calveley. Peter retreated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> without giving battle and fled to Portugal and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. He took refuge with Edward, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>, who restored him the following year after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-najera">Battle of Nájera</a>. The Black Prince left the peninsula with two of Peter's daughters, Constance and Isabella, as hostages for payment, and married them to his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> and Edmund of Langley. Henry returned in September 1368 and the cortes of Burgos recognised him as king. On 14 March 1369 the two armies met at Montiel, where Henry prevailed with du Guesclin's help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Peter took refuge in the fortress of Montiel and negotiated with du Guesclin, offering him 200,000 gold coins and several towns to betray Henry. Du Guesclin told Henry and bargained for more. On the night of 23 March 1369 he led Peter to his tent, where Henry was waiting and killed him with a knife. The body was left unburied for three days.</p><p>Peter's reputation is divided. He was called the Cruel by his enemies and the Just by his admirers, and the chronicler Pero López de Ayala admitted that many regretted his fall, among them peasants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and merchants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a> lamented his death in The Monk's Tale. His death ended the old alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for a time, and his daughter Constance's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> gave England a claim to the Castilian throne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibn al-Khatib, vizier, historian and poet, wrote a celebrated history of the city, the Ihata, and the historian Ibn Khaldun served Muhammad V for a time, travelling on an embassy to <b>Peter I of Castile</b> in 1364.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Black Prince routed the Castilian and French army of Henry of Trastámara at Nájera, restoring <b>Peter of Castile</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Montiel, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.34,-3.7&amp;year=1356" aria-label="Burgos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4460 658 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter of Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 928h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 928h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.34,-3.7&amp;year=1356">What was here in 1356? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
